<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) && role != null => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); this.get(i) == \old(this.get(i)) || (\old(this.get(i)) == null && this.get(i) == null);</code>
--------------------
<code>assert 0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size()) && role != null => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); this.get(i) == (\old(this.get(i)) == null ? null : \old(this.get(i)));</code>

This assertion ensures that if the index is within the valid range and the role is not null, then for every position from the index to the old size of the list, the element at the next position is either null (if the old element at that position was null) or it is equal to the old element at that position.
--------------------
<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) && role != null => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (this.get(i) == null && \old(this.get(i)) == null) || this.get(i) == \old(this.get(i-1));</code>

However, there seems to be a small mistake in the intended logic of the assert statement. The correct logic should compare the new element at position `i` with the old element at position `i`, not `i-1`. Here is the corrected version:

<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) && role != null => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (this.get(i) == null && \old(this.get(i)) == null) || this.get(i) == \old(this.get(i));</code>
--------------------
